WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.683
A web interface can make the assistant accessible across approved devices, while a desktop interface may simplify one-user local operation.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:15.764
Choose according to authentication, network exposure, file handling, support, and accessibility needs.

00:00:15.764 --> 00:00:20.943
Chat history helps continuity but increases privacy and context risk.

00:00:20.943 --> 00:00:30.176
Let users understand what is retained, where it is stored, and how to delete or start a clean session.

00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:34.905
Do not silently place unlimited history into every request.

00:00:34.905 --> 00:00:40.084
System instructions define role, boundaries, format, source use, and escalation.

00:00:40.084 --> 00:00:43.012
Prompt templates support repeatable workflows.

00:00:43.012 --> 00:00:49.993
Neither is a security boundary; users and retrieved content can still influence model behavior.

00:00:49.993 --> 00:00:56.073
Document upload needs file-size, type, malware, privacy, storage, retention, and authorization controls.

00:00:56.073 --> 00:00:59.451
Search should show useful source context.

00:00:59.451 --> 00:01:06.432
Speech and voice features add microphones, audio files, transcription, synthesis, and possibly third-party services.

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:16.115
Usability includes time to first token, response speed, progress feedback, cancellation, errors, citations, copy or export, and recovery after restart.

00:01:16.115 --> 00:01:22.646
A slow assistant with unclear status feels unreliable even when answers are strong.

00:01:22.646 --> 00:01:24.673
Design for correction.

00:01:24.673 --> 00:01:32.554
Users should flag unsupported answers, inspect sources, retry safely, and know when to contact a human.

00:01:32.554 --> 00:01:39.535
The interface should make uncertainty and boundaries visible without burying the workflow in warnings.

00:01:39.535 --> 00:01:42.463
Accessibility is part of usability.

00:01:42.463 --> 00:01:54.398
Keyboard navigation, readable contrast, clear focus, captions or transcripts for audio, understandable errors, and screen-reader-friendly structure determine whether authorized users can operate the assistant reliably.
